    LEN  123 words, 4 sentences
  GOT [run 2/3] (4.5s): That is the Kalam Cosmological Argument, and it has a fatal structural flaw: it begs the question. The premise that the universe "began to exist" is not established by the argument itself; it is simply asserted as a fact to force the conclusion. You cannot use the conclusion (a specific cause) to validate the premise (the beginning of existence) without circular reasoning. Furthermore, even if the universe did have a cause, the leap from "timeless, immaterial, powerful" to "God" is a massive non sequitur. Those attributes describe a metaphysical entity, not necessarily a personal, moral, or divine being. The argument proves, at best, that something caused the universe, not that this something is the God of any specific tradition.
